\begin_layout Section
Literature Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Employer learning researches examine how firms update their assessments of workers as employment reveals information about productivity.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "spence_job_1973"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 examines education as a way to signal ability when employers cannot observe true ability directly.
 Besides,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "farber_learning_1996"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 pay attention to wage dynamics,
 where measures of unobserved productivity should become more strongly associated with wages as experience accumulates.
 Further,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "altonji_employer_2001"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 develop a related test using the NLSY79.
 Using wage regressions containing schooling and productivity proxy,
 they find that the association with the proxy strengthens while the schooling coefficient declines with experience.
 Though these papers differ in regression specifications and predictions for schooling,
 they share the same idea on information acquisition.
 
\end_layout

