\begin_layout Standard
We begin by examining how the relationship among wage,
 schooling,
 and ASVAB evolves over workers’ first three job spells.
 In this section,
 we will show that wage becomes more closely associated with ability as employers learn information about productivity.
 {and whether this relationship varies with employment history.} {Change sentence to recall}We distinguish cumulative labor market experience from tenure with the current employer and estimate the specification separately from the NLSY79 and NLSY97 cohorts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
{Mention these three articles's conclusions} The specification comes from three articles.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "altonji_employer_2001"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 motivate examining whether wages become more closely associated with initially unobserved ability as employers learn,
 while reliance on schooling as a productivity signal declines.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "gibbons_comparative_2005"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 emphasize allowing wage coefficients to vary across occupations,
 reflecting differences in the valuation of worker skills.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "zhang_employer_2009"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 distinguishes cumulative experience from employer tenure,
 where incumbent employers observe performance within the employment,
 whereas outside employers can infer productivity from employment histories.
 The resulting equation is a empirical specifications constructed by these mechanisms.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We first estimate a baseline equation separately for each cohort,
 without allowing wage relationships to vary across occupations.
 We will discuss occupation variation later in this section.
 Following the employer learning motivation of 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citet
key "altonji_employer_2001"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 we allow the associations of schooling and measured ability with wages to change with experience.
 We extend this specification by including interactions with employer tenure:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\log w_{itc}= & \alpha_{kc}+\beta_{Sc}S_{ikc}+\beta_{Zc}Z_{ic}+\gamma_{Sc}\left(S_{ikc}E_{itc}\right)+\gamma_{Zc}\left(Z_{ic}E_{itc}\right)\nonumber \\
 & +\theta_{Sc}\left(S_{ikc}T_{itc}\right)+\theta_{Zc}\left(Z_{ic}T_{itc}\right)+f_{c}\left(E_{itc}\right)+g_{c}\left(T_{itc}\right)\nonumber \\
 & +\lambda_{tc}+X_{itc}\eta_{c}+\varepsilon_{itc}\,.
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 be the worker index,
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 represent calendar years,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c\in\{79,97\}$
\end_inset

 indicate survey cohorts.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $k=k_{it}\in\{1,2,3\}$
\end_inset

 represent worker's current job spell,
 
\begin_inset Formula $j=j_{it}\in\{1,\ldots,J\}$
\end_inset

 represent the current occupation,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{ijtc}$
\end_inset

 indicate the employment in occupation 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here,
 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{itc}$
\end_inset

 is the real hourly wage,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{ikc}$
\end_inset

 is schooling measured at the beginning of spell 
\begin_inset Formula $k=1$
\end_inset

 and held fixed within that job spell,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{ic}$
\end_inset

 is the standardized ASVAB based ability measure,
 which is observed by the researcher but assumed not to be directly observed by employers.
 Actual experience,
 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{itc}$
\end_inset

,
 accumulates across jobs,
 and tenure,
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{itc}$
\end_inset

,
