\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The implied schooling and ability wage gradients are 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
b_{Sc}(E,T) & =\beta_{Sc}+\gamma_{Sc}E+\theta_{Sc}T\,,\\
b_{Zc}(E,T) & =\beta_{Zc}+\gamma_{Zc}E+\theta_{Zc}T\,.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under the informational assumptions of the employer learning model,
 an increasing ability gradient and a decreasing schooling gradient are consistent with employers placing greater weight on observed productivity and less weight on schooling as a signal.
 At fixed tenure,
 these patterns correspond to 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{Zc}>0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{Sc}<0$
\end_inset

.
 During the continuous employment with the same employer,
 experience and tenure both increase.
 The corresponding changes in the gradients are therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{Zc}+\theta_{Zc}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{Sc}+\theta_{Sc}$
\end_inset

.
 These estimates provide descriptive evidence on employer learning,
 as skill accumulation and worker sorting can also change wage gradients.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Including occupational categories,
